{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, Q3, the discriminating half: `The period is never re-measured from the moment an invocation happens to finish` (spec/interaction-events.md:194-198). Its sibling `every-fixed-rate-interval-not-re-measured` delays the handler with a FOREIGN `on_key` body, which pins fixed rate against a scheduler that disarms the handler while it runs -- but not against one that merely re-measures the period from each completion, because that fixture's `every` body is instantaneous, so `completion` and `start` fall on the same tick and no re-measurement can move anything. A QA mutation (re-arm on completion, arrival test left intact) passed it, all 919 fixtures and all 203 test files, which is how the gap was found. Here the `every` body itself is slow: `every 4` with a body that takes 2 ticks, so completion is provably later than the boundary that triggered it and the two clocks separate. Measured: NINE firings under fixed rate, where a completion-re-measured clock fires FIVE. This fixture and its sibling are both needed -- neither catches the mutation the other does.",
